Project Description:
The main purpose of this project is to develop a multi-node IoT-based vehicle safety and accident detection system that can continuously monitor the vehicle's physical condition, cabin environment, location, and visual surroundings.

The system is designed to:

Detect vehicle tilt and abnormal acceleration using MPU6050.
Detect vibration/impact during an accident using SW-420.
Monitor cabin temperature using DHT11.
Detect fire or ignition using an IR flame sensor.
Measure possible structural deformation/crush distance using HC-SR04.
Record the exact accident time using DS1307 RTC.
Obtain the vehicle's GPS location using NEO-6M.
Provide real-time status through an SSD1306 OLED.
Capture live video using an ESP32-CAM.
Send sensor data to Firebase Realtime Database.
Display all parameters on a web-based monitoring dashboard.
Automatically generate an emergency email containing accident details and location when a crash is detected.

Explained Structure:

Node 1 — Master Controller

The Master Controller is based on an ESP32 and functions as the primary telemetry and accident-event detection unit.
It collects:
MPU6050 → acceleration and vehicle orientation
DHT11 → cabin temperature
SW-420 → vibration/impact
IR Flame Sensor → fire detection
DS1307 → accident timestamp

Node 2 — Slave Controller

The Slave Controller handles spatial measurements, GPS information, and local visualization.
It contains:
HC-SR04 → measures distance/deformation
NEO-6M GPS → provides latitude and longitude
SSD1306 OLED → displays system status
The GPS information is particularly important because an accident alert without the accident location would be of limited practical value.

Node 3 — CAM Node

The third node uses an AI-Thinker ESP32-CAM for independent video surveillance.
The ESP32-CAM:
Captures images from the camera.
Converts them into JPEG frames.
Hosts an HTTP video server.
Streams the frames through port 81.
Uses a multipart MJPEG stream.
Sends the live video to the monitoring dashboard.
Keeping the camera node separate prevents the high bandwidth of video streaming from interfering with critical sensor telemetry.

